sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-34> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The abrupt change from increasing to decreasing growth velocity is more likely due to a sudden increase of resistance, such as the formation of a comparatively firm skin during histological differentiation, than to a decline of internal growth forces; the later slowing reflects waning growth forces against rising superficial resistance.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Growth velocity peaks and falls because surface resistance suddenly increases";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter III, “The Rate of Growth”; topic “Conclusion and summary”. Scope: growing organisms generally, including regenerating tissues"
}
